# PowerShell/cmd are happiest with CRLF on Windows. The pin matters beyond
# convenience: it makes these files byte-identical on every platform, which is
# what lets CHECKSUMS.txt hold one hash per file rather than one per OS.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Every .txt here is either a generated twin or a manifest, and all of them
# want LF. plaintext_twins.py writes LF and git stores LF, but without this
# pin checkout applied core.autocrlf and handed the working tree CRLF - so
# every regeneration rewrote the file to LF, `git status` reported a change,
# and `git add` normalized it straight back to nothing (#88). Pinning the
# checkout makes all three agree.
*.txt text eol=lf

# Kept explicit even though *.txt already covers it: the checksum manifest
# must stay LF because `sha256sum -c` treats a trailing CR as part of the
# filename and reports every entry as missing. That is a broken verification,
# not a cosmetic diff, and it should not depend on a glob above it.
CHECKSUMS.txt text eol=lf
releases/*.sha256 text eol=lf

# Release zips are binary and immutable.
releases/*.zip binary
